# tiberius: the strata engine's local patch, against upstream 99f3dbd (v0.1.38).
#
# Five changes, in one patch because they interleave in the same regions of
# expert_source.{hpp,cpp}, prefill.{hpp,cpp}, generate.cpp and gemm.cu.  The
# first four are local and opt-in through their own env var (with every var
# unset the tree behaves like upstream); the fifth is an upstream PR carried
# here until it lands.
#
# 1. STRATA_REGISTER_DAX, the sliding DAX window.  cudaHostRegister cannot pin
#    the 46.84 GiB pack (the driver caps a process's total registered host memory
#    at ~7 GiB, measured: one 6912 MiB registration succeeds, 7168 MiB is refused,
#    disjoint registrations do not escape it, >ReadOnly-only does not help), so
#    the engine registers a 6 GiB window of whole page-aligned layers over the
#    mmap'd pack and slides it as the prefill walk advances; every layer the
#    window holds is DMA-able straight out of DAX, which removes the CPU staging
#    path.  Measured 2026-10-02 on the real corpus: prefill 12,325 -> 9,527 ms
#    (-22.7%) for a 4292-token prompt with the window alone, and 13,136 -> 10,223
#    ms (327 -> 420 tok/s) for the original 0.1.29 version of the change.
#    Needs the fsdax_longterm_pin=1 kernel opt-in
#    (pkgs/kernel-patches/fsdax-longterm-pin.patch).
#
# 2. STRATA_DENSE_FP16, the dense (non-expert) GEMMs on the FP16 tensor cores.
#    Turing has FP16 tensor cores and no BF16 ones, so cuBLAS served the dense
#    GEMMs (482 calls per 4292-token prompt, 335.7 ms) with its FP32 SIMT fallback
#    (magma_sgemmEx_kernel).  The flag converts each operand pair to FP16 in the
#    existing 64 MiB dequantization scratch and calls the same tensor-core path
#    the FP16 projections already use, with FP32 accumulation.  bf16 -> fp16 is
#    exact inside fp16's finite range and the operands measure max|X| 103.5 /
#    max|W| 11.56 against 65504, but the accumulation order changes, so the
#    prompt-path state hash moves and the flag stays opt-in.  Measured: total
#    kernel time 1564 -> 1302 ms (-16.8%), prefill 10,350 -> 9,845 ms (-4.9% mean,
#    -5.8% median) at 4292 tokens and -6.2% at 3514; deployed (same-binary A/B,
#    counterbalanced, 8 runs/arm): +7.23% / +5.89%.
#
# 3. STRATA_REFILL_PIN_SRC, the decode-time VRAM refill read from registered
#    sources.  The adaptive tier's 2 MiB H2D copies came from pageable memory
#    (98% of them) at ~0.87 ms/copy (2.3 GB/s) because the source blobs sit
#    outside the DAX window and the pinned tier, and that cost sat entirely on the
#    decode loop: issue 19.9 + join 18.0 ms of a 122 ms window.  The flag registers
#    each batch's pageable source spans immediately before the copy, MERGING them
#    into disjoint runs first (the driver refuses an H2D copy whose source begins
#    inside a registration and ends outside it - that is the failure an unmerged
#    version produced on every second request), and releases them only once the
#    batch's event has completed.  Measured: refill join 18.04 -> 1.46 ms/window,
#    pageable copy 0.866 -> 0.003 ms, decode 19.61 -> 22.00 tok/s (+12.2%) at 4292
#    tokens and +7.1% at 3514; hit rate, prefill and the warm-up state hash
#    unchanged.
#
# 4. The refill pin's straddle guard (same flag).  An instrumented probe using the
#    engine's exact flags showed the driver refuses any copy whose source begins
#    inside a host registration and ends outside it, with 210 bytes of overflow
#    enough, and both the DAX window's page-rounded edges and the exchange staging
#    can present such blobs.  Each batch's merged spans are now checked against
#    every range the source and the pin path hold: a span that only partly
#    overlaps one is dropped, a blob that still partly overlaps is staged through
#    pinned scratch, a swap whose staging is refused is dropped (a swap is an
#    optimization, the request is not), and a tripwire re-checks after a
#    successful register; a request's pins can no longer outlive it.
#    STRATA_PIN_TRACE=1 logs every decision, STRATA_PIN_FIX=0 restores the old
#    behaviour for diagnostics.  Measured: `--spec-min-p 0.75` aborted every
#    request on a 4344-token decode cell before the fix (7/7, HTTP 400 "an
#    adaptive refill failed", dying in the first adapt round) and passes 10/10
#    after it, while the deployed 0.5 arm is bit-identical (content sha,
#    STATE_HASH, refill counters) and the guard is idle (0 spans dropped, 0 blobs
#    staged).
#
# 5. PR #547 (upstream, carried until it merges): `prefill: bytes_needed counts
#    what carve allocates`.  The prompt path's borrow was computed with a
#    conservative rule (xn counted even when GR-fused, grs always, and the MoE
#    layout for the caller's source), so at chunk 28160 it lent the expert cache
#    ~1.08 GiB (1.15 GB) more than it needed.  Measured 2026-10-03 on the ported
#    tree: borrow
#    6109 -> 5554 slots (11.59 -> 10.51 GiB), prefill -1.77%/-2.59% and pack
#    bytes -2.14%/-1.79% at the 27575 cell (128/256 gen), flat at 4344, decode
#    and the cache hit rate unchanged.  Pure accounting - no numerics, no
#    configuration; upstream #583 carries the same bytes_needed half.
#
# The host expert tier is NOT here: upstream has it natively since 0.1.35 as
# --resident-budget-gib N (which implies --mmap-experts + --resident-cpu-experts),
# used by profiles/nixos/strata.nix together with STRATA_RESIDENT_PIN=0 and
# STRATA_RESIDENT_HEADROOM_GIB=0 to keep the tier pageable and unclamped on this
# 7.4 GiB box.
#
# History: hand-merged 2026-10-01 against 0.1.29 (host tier + window), rebased to
# v0.1.35 on 2026-10-02 (the host tier dropped as upstream-native, dense FP16 and
# the refill pin ported onto the new class surface), to v0.1.38 on 2026-10-03
# (the --peer-device and adaptive-wait seams; the straddle guard added), and
# PR #547 carried the same day.  Every number above is measured on tiberius; the
# raw logs live under /data and the commit messages carry the A/B designs.
#
